    "update_time": null,
    "content": {
     "content_type": "text",
     "parts": [
      "At 1650 rapid, the 6.Bg5 Najdorf is a very sharp line where memorized theory matters a lot, and if you get crushed there regularly, it's reasonable to sidestep it.\n\n**If you want to keep the Najdorf:** the most practical answer to 6.Bg5 at club level is **6…e6 7.f4 Qc7** or **7…Be7** with …Qc7, …Nbd7, …b5. These are more solid than the Poisoned Pawn (7…Qb6) and the plans are easier to learn: castle or keep the king in the center, play …b5–b4 to hit the c3 knight, and don't grab material early.\n\n**If you want something simpler against 1.e4 overall:**\n- **Classical Sicilian (2…Nc6 and …d6 without …a6):** 6.Bg5 is the Richter-Rauzer, also theoretical but more positional.\n- **Taimanov / Kan (2…e6):** flexible, fewer forced lines, less exposure to Bg5 attacks.\n- **Caro-Kann (1…c6):** if you'd rather not play the Sicilian at all; solid, structurally simple.\n\nA middle path: play **2…e6** and go Kan/Taimanov. You keep Sicilian-style counterplay without facing the Bg5 complex."
     ]
    },
